## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first applications with service workers and cross-tab interactions are common in modern web development. This question evaluates practical E2E thinking around offline caching, network replay, idempotent API design, and conflict resolution at a beginner level.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service workers
- Local storage and IndexedDB persistence
- Idempotent server updates with PUT
- Cross-tab concurrency and conflict handling
- Deterministic data seeds for repeatable tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test for offline-first note app
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Offline-first note app', () => {
  test('should create note offline, sync when online, and handle idempotency', async ({ page }) => {
    // Set up deterministic seed for reproducible data
    const noteId = 'test-note-001';
    const noteContent = 'Test note content';

    // Simulate offline by intercepting network requests
    await page.route('**/api/**', (route) => route.abort('failed'));

    // Create note while offline
    await page.goto('/app');
    await page.fill('[data-testid="note-editor"]', noteContent);
    await page.click('[data-testid="save-button"]');

    // Verify note is cached locally
    const localNote = await page.evaluate(() => localStorage.getItem('draft'));
    expect(localNote).toContain(noteContent);

    // Restore network and mock PUT endpoint
    await page.unroute('**/api/**');
    await page.route('**/api/notes/**', (route) => {
      route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ revision: 'rev-123' }) });
    });

    // Trigger sync
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    await expect(page.locator('[data-testid="sync-status"]')).toHaveText('Synced');

    // Verify idempotent behavior - re-submit should not create duplicate
    const initialRequestCount = 1;
    await page.click('[data-testid="sync-button"]');
    // Assert no duplicate entries on server
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes a beginner-friendly yet realistic E2E scenario: draft persistence, UI interactions, and end-to-end verification across multiple pages (feed and profile), with API mocking and test data isolation.

## Key Concepts

- LocalStorage persistence and autosave behavior
- End-to-end API mocking with intercept/stub
- Test data seeding and isolation
- Cross-page assertions and state verification

## Code Example

```javascript
// Cypress example
describe('Drafts and Publish', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    // Seed test user
    cy.request('POST', '/api/test/setup', { user: 'test-user' }).then(() => {
      // Mock the publish API
      cy.intercept('POST', '/api/posts', {
        statusCode: 201,
        body: { id: '123', status: 'published' }
      }).as('publishPost');
    });
    cy.visit('/compose');
  });

  it('autosaves draft, preserves on reload, and publishes to feed and profile', () => {
    // Compose a draft
    cy.get('.rich-text-editor').type('My new post draft');

    // Wait for autosave (15 seconds)
    cy.wait(15000);
    cy.window().then(win => {
      expect(win.localStorage.getItem('draft')).to.exist;
    });

    // Reload and verify draft restoration
    cy.reload();
    cy.get('.rich-text-editor').should('contain', 'My new post draft');

    // Publish the post
    cy.get('[data-testid="publish-btn"]').click();
    cy.wait('@publishPost');

    // Verify draft is cleared
    cy.window().then(win => {
      expect(win.localStorage.getItem('draft')).to.be.null;
    });

    // Verify post appears in main feed
    cy.visit('/feed');
    cy.contains('My new post draft').should('be.visible');

    // Verify post appears on profile
    cy.visit('/profile/test-user');
    cy.contains('My new post draft').should('be.visible');
  });
});
```

## Tool Selection

**Cypress** is recommended for beginners due to its intuitive API, excellent debugging tools, and automatic waiting for elements and commands.

## Test Isolation

Each test runs in a fresh browser context with a clean localStorage state, ensuring no data leaks between tests.

